'''tick<sup>4</sup>''' (tik), ''n.'' [Abbr. of ''ticket.''] 1. Credit; trust: as, to buy on ''tick.''

{{smaller block|I confess my ''tick'' is not good, and I never desire to game for more than I have about me.
{{right|''Sedley'', The Mulberry Garden (1668). (''Nares.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|A poor Wretch that goes on ''tick'' for the paper he writes his Lampoons on, and the very Ale and Coffee that inspires him, as they say.
{{right|''Wycherley'', Love in a Wood, iii. 1.}}}}

2. A score, account, or reckoning.

{{smaller block|Then the bills came down upon me. I tell you there are some of my college ''ticks'' ain't paid now.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Philip, xxxviii.}}}}

{{smaller block|The porter . . . there gave me a little ''ticket'' under his hand as a kind of warrant for mine entertainement in mine Inne.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 57.}}}}

{{smaller block|They send the Beadle with a List of such Friends and Relations as they have a Mind to invite [to the funeral]; and sometimes they have printed ''Tickets'', which they leave at their Houses.
{{right|Quoted in ''Ashton's'' Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne, [I. 55.}}}}

4†. A visiting-card.

{{smaller block|"A ticket?" repeated Cecilia. "Does Lady Nyland only admit her company with tickets?" "O Lord!" cried Miss Larolles, laughing immoderately. "Don't you know what I mean? Why, a ''ticket'' is only a visiting-card with a name}}

