readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|If you ''tickle'' us do we not laugh?
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., iii. 1. 68.}}}}

{{smaller block|Their Stings are not strong enough to enter a Man's Skin; but, if disturbed, they will fly at one as furiously as the great Bees, and will ''tickle'', but cannot hurt you.
{{right|''Dampier'', Voyages, II. ii. 112.}}}}

{{smaller block|We were informed of a very particular manner of catching them by encompassing them with a net, and men go into the water, ''tickle'' them on the belly, and so get them ashoar.
{{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, II. ii. 252.}}}}

{{smaller block|He is playful so out of season that he reminds me of a young lady I saw at Sta. Maria Novella, who at one moment crossed herself, and at the next ''tickled'' her companion.
{{right|''Landor'', Imag. Conv., Southey and Landor, ii.}}}}

2. To touch, affect, or excite agreeably; gratify; please or amuse by gentle appeals to one's imagination, sense of humor, vanity, or the like.

{{smaller block|Whereat her Maiestie laughed as she had bene ''tickled'', and all the rest of the company, although very graciously (as her manner is) she gaue him great thankes.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 217.}}}}

{{smaller block|The first view did even . . . ''tickle'' my senses with inward joy.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 110.}}}}

{{smaller block|How dost like him? art not rapt, art not ''tickled'' now?
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Poetaster, iii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Pleased with a rattle, ''tickled'' with a straw.
{{right|''Pope'', Essay on Man, ii. 276.}}}}

{{smaller block|My father was hugely ''tickled'' with the subtleties of these learned discourses.
{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, iv. 31.}}}}

{{smaller block|The notion of the lion couchant with his currant eyes being hoisted up to the place of honor on a mantle-piece ''tickled'' my hysterical fancy.
{{right|''Mrs. Gaskell'', Cranford, xiv.}}}}

{{smaller block|His spice is of so keen a flavor that it ''tickles'' the coarsest palate.
{{right|''Whipple'', Ess. and Rev., I. 13.}}}}
